WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.350
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
come back to 8.701.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.450
In the previous
lectures, we studied

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.430
how to read Feynman
diagrams, how

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:15.610
to calculate amplitudes
and phase space.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.880
And we are able to use this
using from Fermi's golden rule

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.570
in order to calculate lifetimes
and [INAUDIBLE] cross-sections.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:30.220
We exercise this with a toy
theory and simple examples.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.945
But we focused on leading
order or tree-level diagrams.

00:00:33.945 --> 00:00:35.320
So in this lecture,
I just wanted

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:41.020
to introduce some features of
higher-order diagrams, which

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.400
are rather important.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.430
So we started off
with our toy theory

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:55.270
where we have a primitive
vertex where three particles

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.340
that are going into action.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.150
The strength of the interaction
or the coupling has--

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.650
we just label this as g.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.379
And then we can use
this primitive vertex

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:10.640
in order to build up
scattering processes.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:15.760
And here we want to consider
the process of two particles

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.250
A going to two
particles B. And they

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:23.320
do this by exchanging a particle
C. This is the lowest order

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.290
diagram, or sometimes
called the leading order,

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.950
or sometimes called
the tree-level diagram.

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.190
So how do higher-order
diagrams now look like.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:34.390
Here's the first example.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.370
And this example is one where
one of the legs involved,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:42.510
or one of the particle
involves, has a correction

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.010
to its own mass and energy.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.670
This is the so-called
self-energy diagram.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.590
And if you do the
counting correctly,

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.130
you find that there's
five of those diagrams.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.490
Here shown, we have a
correction of particle A.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.170
But you could also have a
correction of particle C or B

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:04.200
in the outgoing legs and
obviously also in this particle

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.720
here.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.810
The second form of
diagrams is one where you

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.070
correct the vertex involved.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.060
So here there's two
diagrams correcting

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:18.700
each of the vertices, and
so this is shown here.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:22.230
So apparently this changes
then how the vertex actually

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.350
looks to the outgoing legs.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.000
So instead of
directly interacting

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.560
in this primitive vertex, you
have this interaction here

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.720
in those two additional
vertices here.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.200
So this changes
intrinsically how

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.900
the interaction, how the
strength of the interaction

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.580
looks like.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.640
And then there's
the form of diagrams

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.310
which we discussed in the
context of CP violation,

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.280
so-called box diagrams where
you just go around in a box.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.880
That's why they're
called box diagrams.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.610
And apparently also here
you change the strength

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.670
of the interaction involved.

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:03.270
So those are three
varieties of classes

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.400
of higher-order diagrams.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.920
And we will see
much more of those

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.260
when we talk about QED, the weak
interaction, or QCD later on.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.050
The strength of the
individual couplings

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.690
involved and the
particles involved

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.700
changes in how the resulting
features of interaction

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.960
change using those diagrams.

